{"number": 67, "text": "                         Wait      a   minute         -  where's         the     waiter?\n                         Strong        and      weak       vowels\n\n                         The  word London  has two  vowel sounds that are written the\n\n                         same —  London — but pronounced  differently. The first o has a\n                         clear, strong sound, but the second o has a weak sound. Listen\n                         carefully to the difference.\n                         London\n\n\n                    78)  The  word banana  has three vowel sounds which  are written the\n                         same — banana.  The second a has a clear, strong sound, but the\n                         first a and the third a have a weak sound. Listen.       Lao\n                         banana\n\n                         Stressed syllables (see Units 30-32) have strong vowel sounds, but         syllables often have\n                         weak  sounds.\n                         The weak  sound  in London and  banana  is /2/.\n                         London  /'|andan/\n                         banana  /ba'na:na/\n\n                     C8a\n                                     it’s difficult to hear Listen.\n                           London\n                           | have to go to London tomorrow.\n                           banana\n                           Would you like a banana?\n                           This is very important for listening, because it makes it difficult to recognise words. When you speak\n                           English, you don't need to pronounce weak sounds as weakly as this, but it’s important to make a clear\n                           difference between stressed (strong) and unstressed (weak) syllables.\n                     C8b)        to the other weak vowels in these two sentences.\n\n                           ws     WS   WS    W  W  SW\n                           | have to go to London tomorrow.\n                           have - go - Lon - morr are strong, and the others are weak.\n\n                             w    ws     Wwsw\n                           Would you like a banana?\n                           like - na are strong, and the others are weak.\n\n      |B                 Sometimes  different words sound the same because of the weak vowels. Look  at these three\n                         sentences.\n                         Where’s the waiter?\n                         Is this the way to the school?\n                         Can  you wait a minute?\n                         waiter, way to and wait a sound the same: /‘weito/.\n\n                     ©)  Listen to the three sentences and repeat.\n\n\n                66          English Pronunciation in Use (Elementary)", "char_count": 2732, "mean_conf": 90.84, "word_count": 303, "words": [{"text": "y)", "x0": 214, "y0": 296, "x1": 283, "y1": 421, "conf": 41, "bold": false}, {"text": "fe)", "x0": 297, "y0": 295, "x1": 372, "y1": 422, "conf": 44, "bold": false}, {"text": "Wait", "x0": 455, "y0": 298, "x1": 620, "y1": 360, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "a", "x0": 647, "y0": 315, "x1": 682, "y1": 359, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "minute", "x0": 716, "y0": 297, "x1": 958, "y1": 358, "conf": 93, "bold": false}, {"text": "-", "x0": 988, "y0": 328, "x1": 1021, "y1": 339, "conf": 88, "bold": false}, {"text": "where's", "x0": 1051, "y0": 295, "x1": 1309, "y1": 357, "conf": 54, "bold": false}, {"text": "the", "x0": 1337, "y0": 295, "x1": 1449, "y1": 356, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "waiter?", "x0": 1478, "y0": 293, "x1": 1731, "y1": 356, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "Strong", "x0": 456, "y0": 386, "x1": 680, "y1": 462, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "and", "x0": 715, "y0": 385, "x1": 835, "y1": 444, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "weak", "x0": 871, "y0": 384, "x1": 1044, "y1": 463, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "vowels", "x0": 1073, "y0": 382, "x1"